write_note{text:<|"|>Initial investigation focuses on the relationship between the sought quantity and the independence number $\alpha(G)$. The condition requires finding pairs $(u, v)$ such that $d_G(u, v) = 2$ (they share a common neighbor) and $(u, v) \notin E(G)$. Maximizing this quantity suggests maximizing the number of such 2-paths between non-adjacent vertices. A critical constraint is how densely we can pack the graph while maintaining large independent sets (sets of non-friends).

To obtain a strong upper bound or candidate, I explored highly structured graphs. If we consider a complete bipartite graph $K_{k, N-k}$, the non-friends are only within the partitions. If we set $k = N/2 = 228$, the number of pairs of non-friends within one partition set is $\binom{228}{2}$. Each such pair has 228 common neighbors (all vertices in the other partition). Summing over both partitions, the resulting count is $2 \times \binom{228}{2} = 51,756$. This seems to provide a strong candidate for the maximum.<|"|>}

